  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a complex root $x=\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation. Write your answer in the form $a{x}^{2}+bx+c=0,$ where $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are real constants.\n\n{{field-1}}$=0$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}$ is a root, we know that its complex conjugate $x=\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}$ is also a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i}))(x-(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i})-x(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i})+(\\sqrt{2}+3\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}-3\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\sqrt{2}x-\\cancel{3x\\text{i}}-\\sqrt{2}x+\\cancel{3x\\text{i}}+2-9{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}\\,x+2+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}\\,x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
